WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.741 --> 00:00:09.663
There are only two states in the United States of America that are not connected to the others.

2
00:00:10.663 --> 00:00:12.544
One is Hawaii, and the other is.

3
00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:17.065
.Alaska! Alaska is also the largest state in the United States.

4
00:00:17.825 --> 00:00:25.367
It has glaciers and marshland, over 3.5 million lakes, and its capital cannot be reached by car─you have

5
00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:27.688
to take a boat or an airplane to get to Juneau!

6
00:00:28.516 --> 00:00:34.321
During the summers the sun shines on Alaska even at night, due to its location in the far

7
00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:41.446
North. Even though Alaska has these wonderful attractions, it is one of the least populated states in America,

8
00:00:42.127 --> 00:00:45.909
perhaps because of the extreme weather it experiences during the winters.

9
00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.654
Many people aren't aware of Alaska's natural wonders nor its fascinating history.

10
00:00:53.135 --> 00:00:59.564
Most scientists believe that people first arrived in America by walking over the Bering Strait and through Alaska.

11
00:01:00.205 --> 00:01:02.807
You might say, "Wait, that's impossible!

12
00:01:03.428 --> 00:01:04.789
The Bering Strait is water!"

13
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:13.516
But during several ice ages, a bridge of ice over 1,600 kilometers long formed between Siberia and Alaska.

14
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:21.804
The native Alaskans─Inuit, Aleuts, and other groups─crossed this bridge long before Europeans set foot in America.

15
00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:28.237
The Bering Strait is named after one of the first Europeans to travel in the area, a Russian

16
00:01:28.297 --> 00:01:29.518
named Vitus Bering.

17
00:01:30.358 --> 00:01:35.222
In 1741 he was commissioned by the Russian navy to survey the region.

18
00:01:36.103 --> 00:01:41.888
Soon after the arrival of Bering and his crew, others came to take advantage of the natural resources

19
00:01:41.948 --> 00:01:43.149
available in the area.

20
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:50.695
Demand for sea otter pelts led hunters and traders to establish a fur trade on an archipelago off

21
00:01:50.715 --> 00:01:53.725
the west coast of Alaska, called the Aleutian Islands.

22
00:01:54.546 --> 00:02:00.351
The hunters were so greedy that they killed nearly all of the islands' furbearing creatures as they made

23
00:02:00.391 --> 00:02:03.573
their way through the Aleutian Islands toward mainland Alaska.

24
00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:10.039
They also enslaved much of the native population and forced them to hunt animals for the traders.

25
00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:18.466
In 1784 Grigori Shelekhov, a Russian merchant, and two ships full of hunters and traders arrived to set

26
00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:22.673
up the first non-native settlement in Alaska, called Russian America.

27
00:02:23.453 --> 00:02:31.478
Shelekhov then founded a trading company called the Russian-American Company and situated its headquarters in another settlement called

28
00:02:31.538 --> 00:02:33.139
Sitka in 1804.

29
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:40.823
One of the company's managers started the first conservation program in North America for the endangered sea otter.

30
00:02:41.624 --> 00:02:47.647
This program, however, led to less money for Sitka because it limited the number of sea otter pelts

31
00:02:47.727 --> 00:02:48.788
hunters could collect.

32
00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:57.484
The people of Russian America tried to develop other industries, such as coal mining and whaling; unfortunately none

33
00:02:57.504 --> 00:03:00.106
of them generated enough money to stay in business.

34
00:03:00.986 --> 00:03:06.989
Then, when Russia lost the Crimean War in 1856, the czar decided to sell Russian America.

35
00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.772
The United States Secretary of State, William H.

36
00:03:11.872 --> 00:03:14.693
Seward, wanted to secure the land for the U.S.

37
00:03:14.814 --> 00:03:17.235
and agreed to buy Russian America for $7,200,000.

38
00:03:20.288 --> 00:03:26.151
Congress approved the purchase, and the Treaty of Cession of Russian America to the United States was signed

39
00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:28.532
on March 30, 1867.

40
00:03:29.392 --> 00:03:32.973
This legally placed the land under the control of the United States.

41
00:03:33.974 --> 00:03:39.036
Many Americans, however, believed that this was a costly and unnecessary acquisition.

42
00:03:39.916 --> 00:03:46.779
Unhappy with the decision, the public began referring to Alaska as "Seward's Folly" and "Seward's Icebox."

43
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:54.826
Nevertheless American troops watched as the flag was raised at Sitka on October 18, 1867.

44
00:03:56.026 --> 00:04:01.348
After Alaska joined the union, some American companies tried to establish businesses there.

45
00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:06.689
For example, canneries were set up to support Alaska's new salmon fisheries.

46
00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:14.552
In addition Western Union, a communications company, attempted to lay a telegraph line from America all the way

47
00:04:14.632 --> 00:04:19.926
to Moscow, Russia, by going over the Bering Strait and through Siberia in 1866.

48
00:04:21.186 --> 00:04:28.309
Unfortunately they failed to get the telegraph line past the Yukon, a Canadian territory neighboring Alaska on the

49
00:04:28.429 --> 00:04:32.831
east, but their expedition inspired many others to explore Alaska.

50
00:04:33.631 --> 00:04:40.814
The discovery of gold in the Klondike region of the Yukon in 1896 also fueled Americans' curiosity about

51
00:04:40.854 --> 00:04:48.749
Alaska. Fortune-seekers surged toward the Yukon during the Klondike Gold Rush, one of the most well-known in history,

52
00:04:49.309 --> 00:04:51.930
and the fastest way to the Yukon was through Alaska.

53
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.931
Alaska's population nearly doubled in the following decade.

54
00:04:56.671 --> 00:04:59.232
This rise in population forced the U.S.

55
00:04:59.272 --> 00:05:01.112
Congress to take note of Alaska.

56
00:05:02.293 --> 00:05:07.434
In 1912 Congress allowed Alaskans to legislate certain territorial issues.

57
00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:15.357
With this new power, Alaskan natives created the Alaska Native Brotherhood in 1913 to unite their communities.

58
00:05:16.257 --> 00:05:22.301
They petitioned for political and voting rights, integrated classrooms, and other civil rights for native Alaskans.

59
00:05:23.302 --> 00:05:29.445
Despite the economic progress made in the first decade of the twentieth century, Alaska suffered through the Great

60
00:05:29.465 --> 00:05:31.467
Depression with the rest of the United States.

61
00:05:32.387 --> 00:05:37.190
Two of Alaska's most vital resources, fish and copper, declined in value.

62
00:05:38.211 --> 00:05:43.950
Storeowners were making less money, so they either had to lower their employees' wages or fire them.

63
00:05:45.031 --> 00:05:49.493
More than half of Alaska's workforce lost their jobs during the Great Depression.

64
00:05:50.874 --> 00:05:54.956
When World War II began, Alaska's employment rate skyrocketed.

65
00:05:55.816 --> 00:06:03.921
Over 150,000 military personnel were stationed in newly built military installations in Alaska due to its proximity to

66
00:06:03.961 --> 00:06:05.802
Japan. The U.S.

67
00:06:05.822 --> 00:06:09.864
government also built the Alaska Highway as a military supply road.

68
00:06:10.704 --> 00:06:18.026
Recognizing Alaska's military importance, the Japanese bombed and occupied the Aleutian Islands in June of 1942.

69
00:06:18.906 --> 00:06:24.188
This was the only part of North America to be invaded and occupied during the Second World War.

70
00:06:24.988 --> 00:06:30.589
Alaska had already applied for statehood once, but it wasn't granted statehood until after U.S.

71
00:06:30.629 --> 00:06:33.670
officials saw how strategic its location truly was.

72
00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:38.591
So with the cold war looming, and Alaska being the closest U.S.

73
00:06:38.611 --> 00:06:41.593
territory to the Soviet Union, President Dwight D.

74
00:06:41.713 --> 00:06:47.857
Eisenhower signed the law that allowed Alaska to become the 49th state of the United States of America

75
00:06:48.438 --> 00:06:50.639
on January 3, 1959.

76
00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.882
Alaska's good fortune did not last long, however.

77
00:06:55.763 --> 00:06:59.546
On March 27, 1964, the newest U.S.

78
00:06:59.566 --> 00:07:03.128
state was hit by one of the most powerful earthquakes ever recorded.

79
00:07:03.949 --> 00:07:11.639
The Good Friday Earthquake, as it was called, killed 131 people and created mudslides and tsunamis that destroyed

80
00:07:11.679 --> 00:07:13.781
the port towns of Valdez and Cordova.

81
00:07:14.721 --> 00:07:18.104
Both cities were rebuilt on higher ground after the earthquake.

82
00:07:18.905 --> 00:07:25.231
Valdez also suffered another disaster when the Exxon Valdez, an oil tanker, crashed on a nearby reef.

83
00:07:26.171 --> 00:07:33.138
Oil spilled over nearly two thousand kilometers of the coastline and affected much of the area's wildlife, particularly

84
00:07:33.218 --> 00:07:37.300
salmon, sea otters, seals, and sea birds.

85
00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.283
The oil industry has been a cause for concern in Alaska since oil was discovered in the state

86
00:07:42.623 --> 00:07:43.364
in 1968.

87
00:07:44.544 --> 00:07:52.109
Alaskans managed to construct the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System, which transports oil from Prudhoe Bay over eight hundred miles

88
00:07:52.169 --> 00:07:56.691
to the rebuilt city of Valdez, without disturbing the land or wildlife surrounding it.

89
00:07:57.512 --> 00:08:04.376
Unfortunately the large oil reserves that were found beneath the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, a protected area of

90
00:08:04.436 --> 00:08:07.341
Alaska, cannot be accessed without drilling.

91
00:08:08.461 --> 00:08:13.082
This disruption would negatively affect many of the species that are living in the preserve.

92
00:08:14.003 --> 00:08:19.224
Since the late 1970s, no one has been able to come up with a solution to this problem.

93
00:08:20.745 --> 00:08:24.646
Alaskans are still struggling to coexist peacefully with their environment.

94
00:08:25.466 --> 00:08:31.528
Most want to utilize their natural resources to further develop their state and improve the lives of its

95
00:08:31.608 --> 00:08:36.283
residents. Others think that the natural beauty of Alaska should be preserved.

96
00:08:37.044 --> 00:08:43.811
These people think that Alaska's wildlife and awe-inspiring scenery will attract tourists and ensure its economic future.

97
00:08:44.771 --> 00:08:51.158
The decisions today's leaders make will shape the future of Alaska, just as past leaders have made Alaska

98
00:08:51.238 --> 00:08:52.078
what it is today.
